					<div class="article__inner">
									<div class="article__content">
							
    <section class="main-content">
                    <div class="main-content__intro margin-trim type-body-xl">
                <p><span style="font-weight: 400;">A rare revival of one of </span><b>Arthur Miller</b><span style="font-weight: 400;">’s most unflinching and urgent plays.</span></p>

            </div>
        
                    <div class="main-content__text margin-trim type-body-l">
                <p><span style="font-weight: 400;">France. 1942. A group of people are detained. Picked up off the street without explanation, they cling to the hope that this is nothing more than a routine identity check. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Over the course of an hour, as one by one they are called away for questioning, a terrible truth begins to emerge: they have been selected not at random, but for who they are. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Rarely performed, </span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Incident at Vichy</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;"> confronts our complicity and responsibility in the face of injustice. Olivier and Tony Award-nominated director <strong>Melly Still</strong> (<em>Coram Boy,</em> <em>My Brilliant Friend, Morning &#8217;til Midnight, Revenger&#8217;s Tragedy</em>: National Theatre) returns to Arcola following her sold-out production of <em>Sputnik Sweetheart</em>. </span></p>

            </div>
            </section>

